        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Does Rogue Make a Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>No. Rogue Fitness resells treadmills made by Woodway, TrueForm and STEPR. The one called a
            "Rogue treadmill" is the Rogue | Woodway Curve LTG, a Woodway Curve LTG in Rogue branding, at $3,495
            on sale on 5 November 2026. For most home buyers the choice at Rogue is between that and the
            TrueForm Trainer at the same price.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Rogue's treadmill category listed eight machines on the day we checked. Five are curved manual
            treadmills — belts you drive with your own stride, with no motor and no outlet — and that is what
            Rogue is known for, because its customers are mostly CrossFit, HYROX and garage-gym athletes who
            want sprint and conditioning work. Two are motorized STEPR treadmills with fast speed changes and
            steep incline, and one is Woodway's compact motorized Vive at $11,395.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buying through Rogue does not, as far as we could find, make any of them cheaper or more expensive
            than buying from the maker: the Curve LTG, the TrueForm Trainer and the STEPR TREADSLED were at the
            same prices on Rogue, on the maker's store or at another dealer on the day. What Rogue adds is a
            single checkout for a whole gym, its own customer service and, on the Curve LTG, its badge. What it
            does not sell is the <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner</a>,
            the best-known curved treadmill outside the CrossFit world: Rogue lists Assault's air bike but no
            AssaultRunner was in its treadmill category. We have not tested any of these machines.
          </p>
        </div>

