              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Footprint</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">67 x 34 x 70 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">64 x 31 x 63 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Curve</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 3%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TrueForm's shallow curve</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">LCD v2, Bluetooth and ANT+ heart rate, group mode</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">LCD, Bluetooth FTMS for apps</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Choose the Curve LTG if</h3>
          <p>
            You are a runner of average build who wants the longer deck. Eight extra inches of running surface
            matters on a curved treadmill, because your speed depends on where on the curve you plant your
            foot; a longer deck gives more room to drift back and recover. You also get Woodway's slat belt,
            which Woodway describes as shock-reducing, and the group mode lets up to four people track stats
            in a class or family. Owners on Rogue's page say the LTG takes noticeably more effort to move than
            an AssaultRunner. That is useful for conditioning, less so if you want easy long runs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Choose the TrueForm Trainer if</h3>
          <p>
            You weigh more than 350 lb, want to ride Zwift or log to Strava from the treadmill itself, or are
            short of space. The Trainer's 400 lb rating is the highest of any curved treadmill at this price
            that we have checked, and its display pairs directly with FTMS apps. TrueForm's selling point is
            form feedback: its shallow curve and belt are meant to slow you when you overstride or sit back.
            The trade is the shorter 54-inch surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best curved treadmills</a>
            roundup sets both against the AssaultRunner, SOLE C80, Tru Grit and others outside Rogue, and our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>
            explains how the curve itself changes the workout.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curved-or-motorized" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Curved Manual or Motorized: Which Rogue Treadmill Type?</h2>
<p>
            Most of Rogue's treadmill range is curved manual machines, and they are not a substitute for a
            normal treadmill for everyone. A curved treadmill has no motor: you push the belt with your feet,
            and the further up the front of the curve you run, the faster it goes. That makes it excellent for
            sprints and intervals, because you can change speed instantly with no buttons, and it builds a
            forefoot, upright running style. It makes steady easy running harder than on a motorized belt, and
            there is no incline and no way to set a pace and hold it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pick a curved manual treadmill</strong> (Curve LTG, TrueForm, TREADSLED) for sprints,
            HIIT, CrossFit and HYROX work, garages without a spare circuit, and athletes who already run
            outdoors.</li>
            <li><strong>Pick a motorized treadmill</strong> (STEPR TREAD PRO or XL) for long steady runs,
            marathon training, incline walking and anyone new to running, who will find a manual belt tiring
            within minutes.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs.
            motorized treadmill guide</a> sets out the trade-offs, and
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-manual-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a manual
            treadmill</a> covers the technique that makes a curved belt feel natural. If you are buying for
            HIIT on a motorized machine, see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-hiit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for HIIT</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">For HYROX and sled work</h3>
          <p>
            If you are training for HYROX or want resisted pushes, the STEPR TREADSLED XL VPR is the machine in
            Rogue's line built for it, with a dedicated sled-push mode. A cheaper route is a curved treadmill
            with heavy magnetic resistance levels; our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a>
            page covers sled-style sessions. The official HYROX race treadmill is the Centr x HYROX Perform
            Tread, which Rogue does not sell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-from-rogue" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Buying a Treadmill From Rogue: Shipping, Warranty and Price</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Price</h3>
          <p>
            We compared Rogue's prices on 5 November 2026 with the makers' and other dealers'. The Curve LTG
            at $3,495 matched the sale price on Woodway's own store when our Woodway guide checked it; the
            TrueForm Trainer at $3,495 matched TrueForm's rubber-tread price; the STEPR TREADSLED XL VPR at
            $5,999.99 matched STEPR and StrengthWarehouseUSA; and the TREAD PRO at $3,999.99 matched Peak Primal
            Wellness. Only the TrueForm Runner needs care, because TrueForm prices it by configuration from
            $6,195 to $10,195 and Rogue showed one price of $6,595. Shop the exact frame, tread and display you
            want. These are large purchases, and Rogue lists a financing option; our
            <a href="/treadmill-financing/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill financing guide</a>
            explains how to read the terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shipping</h3>
          <p>
            Rogue's Curve LTG page showed free shipping and "Cont. US 48 only". These are 300 to 350 lb
            machines delivered by freight, typically to the curb or garage rather than carried into a room.
            One owner on Rogue's page called the delivery experience "mid", and another said a semi needed room
            to unload. Ask before ordering whether inside delivery is available, and read our
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a>
            for what to arrange.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty</h3>
          <p>
            The warranty is the maker's, not Rogue's. Rogue's pages stated it for the STEPR machines (10 years
            on the frame for all three we read, with 2 to 3 years on parts and 2 on labour), but not on the
            Curve LTG or TrueForm pages we read. Woodway lists 5 years on the Curve LTG's frame, 3 on the belt
            and moving parts and 1 on wear items and labour; our TrueForm guide covers TrueForm's terms. Ask
            Rogue who handles a claim: one Curve LTG owner on Rogue's page said Rogue arranged for a Woodway
            technician to fix a noisy bearing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains what to look for.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database on 5 November 2026. Rogue
            Fitness (Coulter Ventures) has had recalls of Rogue home timers in 2020 for a fire hazard,
            MobilityWOD door anchors in 2015 and barbell brackets in 2011; none involves a treadmill. A 2023
            recall of "Rogue" hoverboards was by Jetson, an unrelated company. We found no recalls for
            Woodway, TrueForm or STEPR.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Space, Floors and Setup</h2>
<p>
            Curved manual treadmills are heavy and long for what they are. The Curve LTG is 67 inches long on
            Rogue's figure (Woodway's own documents go up to 76), the TrueForms are 64 inches, and the STEPR
            TREADSLED is nearly 81 inches by 45 wide. Add room behind the machine: a curved treadmill can spit
            you off the back if you stop suddenly, so keep at least the clearance our
            <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills in a garage</a>
            recommends.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of the curved machines needs an outlet, which is a real advantage in a garage, but they put
            300-plus pounds on a small footprint. Put them on a rubber floor or a mat to protect the floor and
            reduce noise; slat belts are louder at a sprint than a cushioned motorized deck. The motorized
            STEPR TREAD PRO and XL need a nearby outlet, and the Woodway Vive a dedicated 20-amp circuit. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for a garage</a>
            roundup covers temperature, dust and power in an unheated space.
          </p>
          <p>
            Assembly is moderate: owners on Rogue's Curve LTG page describe it as "a slight pain" with poor
            instructions but manageable by two people. Most of the weight is in the frame and slat bed, which
            arrive largely assembled, so the work is mainly fitting the uprights and console and getting the
            machine level. Get help to move it into place; these are not machines one person should tip onto
            their transport wheels alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outside-rogue" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Curved Treadmills Rogue Does Not Sell</h2>
<p>
            Rogue's range is good but not complete. If you are shopping for a curved treadmill, compare these
            before you check out:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner Pro</a>:
            the best-known curved treadmill, about $3,499 at US dealers, 350 lb rating, sold through Assault
            Fitness and other dealers, not Rogue.</li>
            <li><a href="/bells-of-steel-blitz-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bells of Steel Blitz</a>:
            a curved treadmill with six magnetic resistance levels and a 400 lb rating, $4,749.99 at Bells
            of Steel on the day.</li>
            <li><a href="/nohrd-sprintbok/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NOHrD Sprintbok</a>: a
            German curved treadmill with a solid-wood frame and wooden slats, from $8,199 in the US.</li>
            <li>The SOLE C80 and <a href="/tru-grit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Tru Grit Runner</a>: curved treadmills nearer $2,000 to $2,500, both in our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a broader view of non-motorized options, including flat-belt manual treadmills that cost a few
            hundred dollars, see our <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best
            manual treadmills</a>. If what you want is a commercial motorized treadmill rather than a curved
            one, our <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmills</a>
            guide covers the club brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Rogue make its own treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Rogue Fitness resells treadmills from Woodway, TrueForm and STEPR. The Rogue | Woodway Curve LTG is a Woodway Curve LTG with exclusive Rogue branding, and Rogue calls it the official runner of CrossFit and the CrossFit Games. Every other treadmill Rogue sells carries its maker’s name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is the Rogue Woodway treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 5 November 2026 Rogue listed the Rogue | Woodway Curve LTG at $3,495, down from $3,995, with free shipping to the contiguous 48 states. That matched the sale price on Woodway’s own store when we checked it. Prices change, so check Rogue’s page before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the Rogue curved treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rogue sells several curved manual treadmills: the Rogue | Woodway Curve LTG, the TrueForm Trainer and TrueForm Runner, and the STEPR TREADSLED XL VPR. The one usually meant by "Rogue curved treadmill" is the Curve LTG, the Rogue-branded Woodway used at the CrossFit Games. All are self-powered and need no outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Rogue Woodway Curve LTG the same as the Woodway Curve LTG?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mechanically it is Woodway’s Curve LTG with Rogue branding in black, as Rogue’s listing and Treadmill Review Guru describe it. Rogue lists the same 350 lb running rating, 62 by 17-inch running surface and roughly 3% curve as Woodway, and the price matched Woodway’s sale price. It is sold only through Rogue.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Rogue sell the AssaultRunner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on 5 November 2026: Rogue’s treadmill category listed Woodway, TrueForm and STEPR machines but no AssaultRunner, although Rogue does sell Assault’s air bike. The AssaultRunner Pro was about $3,499 through Assault and other US dealers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Rogue treadmill is best for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home athletes, the TrueForm Trainer or the Rogue | Woodway Curve LTG, both $3,495 on sale. Pick the Curve LTG for its longer 62-inch deck and the TrueForm for its 400 lb rating and app connectivity. If you want set paces and incline for long runs, the motorized STEPR TREAD PRO at $3,999.99 is the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Rogue treadmills need electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The curved manual models do not: the Curve LTG, both TrueForms and the STEPR TREADSLED are self-powered, with battery or generator consoles. The motorized STEPR TREAD PRO, TREAD XL and All-In Tread XL need an outlet, and the Woodway Vive needs a dedicated 115 V, 20-amp circuit, according to Rogue.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Rogue Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These are expensive machines bought largely on reputation. The errors we see are about fit, not quality.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a curved manual treadmill for easy running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Curved manual treadmills are superb for sprints and intervals and hard work for long, steady runs, because you power every step and cannot set a pace. If most of your treadmill time will be easy miles or incline walking, a motorized machine such as the STEPR TREAD PRO is the right Rogue purchase.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the weight rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Curve LTG is rated for 350 lb running, the TrueForm Trainer for 400 lb, and Rogue left the STEPR TREADSLED’s rating blank. Heavier runners should choose the Trainer or confirm the figure in writing before ordering; a curved belt loads the bearings hard at a sprint.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Rogue is cheaper than the maker</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">On the day we checked, Rogue matched the makers’ prices rather than beating them. Compare the TrueForm Runner especially, which TrueForm prices by configuration, and check whether a sale on the maker’s store or another dealer is running before you buy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning for the wrong length</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Woodway’s documents give the Curve LTG’s length as anything from about 67 to 76 inches. Measure for the longest figure plus clearance behind, and remember the STEPR TREADSLED is nearly 81 inches long and 45 wide.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

